2026-11-16 13:29:51 | RESPONSE | sactor.llm:160 | **Analysis of the unsafe operations**

1. `*arr.offset(high as isize)` reads the pivot through a raw pointer. Crown reports `arr` as a mutable, transient array pointer, so it is a borrowed, mutable slice. It becomes `arr: &mut [i32]`, and the read becomes `arr[high as usize]`, which is bounds-checked.
2. `*arr.offset(j as isize)` is a raw read in the loop. It becomes `arr[j as usize]`.
3. `swap(arr.offset(i), arr.offset(j))` creates two `&mut i32` into the same buffer. Safe Rust cannot hold both at once, and when `i == j` they would alias. `slice::swap(i, j)` does the same job safely and handles `i == j`. I use it instead of the provided `swap` helper.

The function needs no `unsafe`. The signature keeps `low` and `high` as `i32` so the return value and behavior match the original.

----FUNCTION----
```rust
pub fn partition(arr: &mut [i32], low: i32, high: i32) -> i32 {
    let pivot = arr[high as usize];
    let mut i = low;
    let mut j = low;
    while j < high {
        if arr[j as usize] <= pivot {
            arr.swap(i as usize, j as usize);
            i += 1;
        }
        j += 1;
    }
    arr.swap(i as usize, high as usize);
    i
}
